---
/**
 * The Veritas case study.
 *
 * Same split as the others: the framing above the provenance note is written
 * here for a portfolio reader; everything below it is fetched from the README
 * on the repository's default branch, which is master, at build time. If the
 * fetch fails or a marker has gone, `fetchCaseStudySection` throws and this
 * page fails to build; see src/lib/case-study-readme.ts.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseShot from '../../components/CaseShot.astro';
import { fetchCaseStudySection } from '../../lib/case-study-readme';
import { projectByHref } from '../../lib/projects';

const { html, sourceUrl } = await fetchCaseStudySection({
  owner: 'VelaWind',
  repo: 'veritas',
  branch: projectByHref('/projects/veritas')?.branch,
  expectedHeading: '# Veritas',
});

const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/VelaWind/veritas';
const liveUrl = 'https://veritas-delta-pearl.vercel.app';
---

<Base
  sky={0.35}
  title="Veritas · VelaWind"
  type="article"
  description="A catalogue of knowledge claims where Postgres enforces the rules, not the application."
>
  <main class="page" id="main">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/">VelaWind</a> · <a href="/projects">Projects</a></p>

    <h1>Veritas</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A catalogue of knowledge claims where Postgres enforces the rules, not the
      application.
      <br />
      <a href={liveUrl}>Live site</a> · <a href={repoUrl}>Source</a>
    </p>

    <CaseShot href="/projects/veritas" />

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A catalogue of claims is only worth reading if its rules hold. In Veritas
      every hypothesis has an epistemic status and a confidence score, and the
      two have to agree: a speculation cannot sit at 90. A confidence change has
      to carry a reason. The history of changes cannot be rewritten afterwards.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those rules have many writers to survive. The admin pages, the API
      routes, contributors proposing changes, maintenance scripts holding the
      service key, and, later, AI research agents that read sources and propose
      hypotheses and evidence. Each writer is a place where a rule can be
      skipped.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the obvious approach was not used</h2>
    <p>
      The obvious place for the rules is the application: validate every write
      in the route that handles it. That holds only for writers that go through
      those routes, and it holds only as long as every new route remembers to
      do it. A script with the service key, a route added next year, or an
      agent runner with a bug all write without passing that check.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the rules live in PostgreSQL. A CHECK constraint binds status to
      confidence. A trigger refuses a confidence change without a rationale and
      writes the history row itself. The history tables have no update or delete
      policy, so under row-level security no application role can alter them.
      The application still validates, but only to give a readable error
      message; the database is what refuses.
    </p>
    <p>
      The agents were added later and were given no new way to write. They
      propose into the same queue a human contributor uses, an admin approves
      every proposal, and a trigger enforces each agent's quota and domain scope
      even if the runner skips its own limits. The worst a prompt injection in a
      source can produce is a pending proposal that a reviewer rejects. The
      dashboard follows the same idea: its figures come from a database view
      read under the viewer's own row-level security, so they cannot go stale.
    </p>

    <h2>One trade-off</h2>
    <p>
      The query layer does not use generated database types. It casts query
      results to hand-written types in 13 places, so a select whose shape stops
      matching those types still compiles. That is deliberate: regenerating the
      types can never break the build. The cost is that this class of mismatch
      is found at runtime, by the smoke run and the verification scripts, not by
      the compiler.
    </p>

    <h2>What should change</h2>
    <p>
      The worst failure in the project's history was not a crash. The deployed
      site served every page with HTTP 200 for about two months while its
      database was unreachable: a placeholder database URL ended up in the
      production build, every query failed, and every page rendered a normal
      looking empty state. The project's decision log records it as the third
      instance of one shape, a wrong value producing a plausible success. A
      production build now refuses the placeholder, a failed query raises once a
      real database is configured, and a smoke run checks the deployed site
      every 30 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      A dependency upgrade found the same shape again. Moving the validation
      library to its next major version produced no type error, but the new
      version fills in defaults for fields a request leaves out, so an edit to a
      hypothesis title alone would have wiped its assumptions and open
      questions, and an empty note edit would have unpublished the note. Edits
      now apply defaults only to fields the caller sent. Every schema was run on
      the same 7,415 inputs under both versions with no difference in what is
      accepted or produced, and a unit test fails if the old behaviour returns.
    </p>
    <p>
      The scripts that exercise the database end to end used to run by hand,
      because they write to it and there was one database. CI now starts a
      throwaway Postgres inside the runner, applies every migration and the
      seed, and runs them there on every pull request, with a guard that stops
      the job unless every target is on the runner itself. The search rate
      limit moved into the database the same way: one atomic function counts
      each client across every server instance, and a test sends 60
      simultaneous requests at a limit of 30 and expects exactly 30 through.
    </p>
    <p>
      What remains: unit tests cover five modules, with no component or browser
      tests, and that CI database runs on local images rather than the hosted
      platform. The research graph can be driven by keyboard, but it was tested
      in Chromium only, not with a screen reader.
    </p>

    <p class="provenance">
      The rest of this page is fetched from
      the <a href={sourceUrl}>Veritas README</a> at build time and rendered here,
      so it is maintained in the repository and not in this site. It covers the
      architecture, how it is verified and, under "Known limits", what does not
      work.
    </p>

    <div class="readme" set:html={html} />
  </main>
</Base>
